# gRPC server for GC communication
MC_GRPC_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:50052
# Health endpoint
MC_HEALTH_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8081
# WebTransport server for client connections
MC_WEBTRANSPORT_BIND_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:4433
# Deployment region (local for Kind)
MC_REGION=local
# GC service discovery via Kubernetes DNS
GC_GRPC_URL=http://gc-service.dark-tower:50051
# AC JWKS endpoint for JWT validation
AC_JWKS_URL=http://ac-service.dark-tower:8082/.well-known/jwks.json
# TLS certificate paths for WebTransport (mounted from mc-service-tls Secret)
MC_TLS_CERT_PATH=/etc/mc-tls/tls.crt
MC_TLS_KEY_PATH=/etc/mc-tls/tls.key
# ---- OpenTelemetry (R-55, task #6) ----
# MC's OTel SDK is wired into main.rs via init_otel (R-55), gated by an
# explicit boolean OTEL_ENABLED (NOT presence of OTLP_ENDPOINT). This is the
# prod base, so OTEL_ENABLED defaults to "false" here; the Kind overlay
# patches it to "true" for dev. Enabling requires OTEL_ENABLED=true PLUS the
# MC->collector egress NetworkPolicy rule on :4317 (now present) PLUS a
# reachable collector — init_otel probes eagerly and FAILS HARD at startup if
# the collector is unreachable (pod fails readiness; mirrors GC's task #26 /
# AC's task #25 exemplars).
OTEL_ENABLED=false
# OTLP-gRPC endpoint for the in-cluster OTel collector (R-59) — MC's OWN
# span-export endpoint. The http:// scheme is required (parsed via tonic
# Endpoint::from_shared — a bare host:port fails at init). Only consumed when
# OTEL_ENABLED=true. Namespace is dark-tower (NOT default).
OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector.dark-tower:4317
# Head-sampling ratio in [0.0, 1.0]; init_otel is the authoritative validator.
OTEL_SAMPLE_RATE=1.0
# deployment.environment resource attribute (ADR-0011). NOTE: this prod base
# hardcodes "development"; prod OTel graduation MUST overlay-patch this so
# prod telemetry is not mistagged (same tracked caveat as GC's task #26 —
# see docs/TODO.md).
DEPLOYMENT_ENVIRONMENT=development
# ==========================================================================
# Client-facing media signalling (ADR-0036 §5, §6)
#
# EVERY KEY IN THIS BLOCK THAT AN IMAGE READS IS REQUIRED. mc-service reads
# them via ConfigError::MissingEnvVar with NO Rust default, so a missing key
# CrashLoopBackOffs the pod rather than silently reverting to a value nobody
# chose. That is deliberate: a Rust-defaulted key wired into mc-0 and
# forgotten on mc-1 passes `dt-guard env-config` green while the two MC
# instances behave differently. The documented default is the value below —
# this file is the source, not a constant in config.rs.
#
# The video and audio ENCODING parameters MC directs no longer live here: they
# are the shared `media-codec-profile` (infra/services/media-codec-profile/
# profile.env), which MH also reads. Only MC-only knobs belong in this file.
#
# ROLLOUT ORDERING. This ConfigMap is a `configMapGenerator` (ADR-0038 §2,
# content-addressed): a VALUE EDIT here renames the ConfigMap, changes both
# Deployments' pod templates, and ROLLS mc-0 AND mc-1 on the next apply of the
# environment root. It is never a hot toggle.
#
# THE COUPLED SET IS THREE ARTIFACTS, NOT TWO: this ConfigMap, BOTH
# Deployments, and the image. The Deployment is in the set because it carries
# the `configMapKeyRef` — it is what declares that a pod CONSUMES a key; this
# file only supplies the value. A key present here but unreferenced there is
# not consumed at all. Add a key (and its refs) with-or-before the image that
# reads it; remove a read one deploy before its key (the retirement banners
# below), never in the same deploy.
#
# See docs/runbooks/mc-deployment.md §Config-failure triage: a new Deployment
# against an old ConfigMap gives CreateContainerConfigError (empty logs,
# `describe` names the key), while a new image against an old pod template
# gives CrashLoopBackOff (`logs --previous` names the variable) — opposite
# first steps.
#
# (Corrected 2026-10-20: this block previously said ConfigMaps were listed
# under `resources:`, not `configMapGenerator:`, so editing this file did not
# roll the pods. ADR-0038 §2 made that false; every MC ConfigMap is generated.)
# ==========================================================================
# Per-kind receive-slot caps (story 3 R-1, R-26; ADR-0036 §6). Each kind has
# its own server-side cap, and a client declaring more slots of a kind than
# its cap is TRUNCATED to the cap: each excess slot is reported in the
# permanent `SLOT_STATE_UNAVAILABLE` state (proto/dark_tower/signaling/v1/
# signaling.proto), echoing the declared slot_id with no sender and no url —
# visible, never a silent participant, and NEVER A JOIN FAILURE. This
# supersedes story 2 R-1's reject-the-whole-declaration rule (the retired
# `MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS` below still enforces that rule until MC-A lands).
#
# Maximum AUDIO receive slots per client.
# THE VALUE ITSELF IS BOUNDED (bounds in crates/mc-service/src/config.rs, the
# per-kind constants added by MC-A) and mc-service refuses to start outside
# them. The floor is not zero: an audio cap of 0 would mean no client can hear
# anyone — a silent total outage that reads as valid configuration. The
# ceiling keeps the property ADR-0036 §6 relies on this cap for
# ("resource-amplification-by-request becomes structurally impossible rather
# than rate-limited"); a cap whose own value is unbounded is not a cap.
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, at receive-capability validation, once
# MC-A lands. The current image ignores this key.
# Where an operator sees it: `mc_media_receive_slot_cap{media_kind="audio"}`
# (the value the pod enforces) — but UNTIL MC-A LANDS that series echoes the
# retired `MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS` below, not this key: an edit here moves
# nothing an operator can read until then.
MC_MAX_AUDIO_RECEIVE_SLOTS=8
# Maximum VIDEO receive slots per client. Bounded like the audio cap (bounds
# in crates/mc-service/src/config.rs, added by MC-A), EXCEPT that 0 is
# deliberately legal: it is the R-26 VIDEO-OFF LEVER.
#
# THE LEVER AND ITS BLAST RADIUS. Setting 0 truncates every video slot to
# unavailable, so no publisher is watched and no camera is directed to send;
# audio is unaffected. It applies to declarations made after the new MC pods
# are up. Because this ConfigMap is content-addressed it ROLLS BOTH MC PODS
# (one restart per pod's meetings) — it is not a hot toggle. It is deliberately
# NOT in `media-codec-profile`, so pulling it never rolls MH (an MH roll sheds
# every media session on the pod). The operator procedure, deploy order and
# rollback live in docs/runbooks/mc-deployment.md; this comment does not
# duplicate them.
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, at receive-capability validation, once
# MC-A lands. The current image ignores this key.
# Where an operator sees it: `mc_media_receive_slot_cap{media_kind="video"}`
# (series added with MC-A). A reading of 0 confirms the lever applied.
MC_MAX_VIDEO_RECEIVE_SLOTS=4
# RETIRED — PENDING REMOVAL. Do not tune, do not "fix".
#
# Superseded by the per-kind caps above, `MC_MAX_AUDIO_RECEIVE_SLOTS` and
# `MC_MAX_VIDEO_RECEIVE_SLOTS`. This key is a TOTAL across kinds.
#
# WHAT IS TRUE NOW: the CURRENT image still READS AND ENFORCES it exactly as
# before — a declaration over this total is REJECTED WHOLE (never clamped),
# counted on mc_media_receive_capability_declarations_total
# {outcome="slot_count_over_cap"}, and a value outside its bound
# (`MIN_RECEIVE_SLOTS`/`MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS`, crates/mc-service/src/config.rs)
# fails startup. The current image also publishes it as
# `mc_media_receive_slot_cap{media_kind="audio"}`. Read removed by MC-A; after
# that nothing reads this key.
#
# WHY IT IS STILL HERE (two-deploy retirement): the read goes one deploy before
# the key. Both refs (mc-0/mc-1-deployment.yaml) are `optional: true` so a
# later deploy can delete key and refs together without any reachable revision
# holding a hard ref to a missing key. Scheduled removal: docs/TODO.md §Media
# Path Obligations, "Delete the story-3 retired config keys".
MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS=8
# Maximum ACCEPTED receive-capability declarations per WebTransport
# connection. Bounds client-driven O(N) roster reads on the shared meeting
# actor: a client alternating between two valid declarations would otherwise
# drive one roster clone per message. Charged ONLY on acceptance — every
# rejection is decided without touching the meeting actor, so a client looping
# rejects cannot bypass it. Bounded at load (crates/mc-service/src/config.rs).
# 64 is generous for legitimate layout changes over a long session.
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, per connection.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_receive_capability_declarations_total
# {outcome="declaration_budget_exhausted"}.
MC_MAX_RECEIVE_CAPABILITY_DECLARATIONS=64
# RETIRED — PENDING REMOVAL (all three MC_AUDIO_* keys). Do not tune.
#
# Superseded by `MEDIA_AUDIO_CODEC`, `MEDIA_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS` and
# `MEDIA_AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_HZ` in the shared media-codec-profile
# (infra/services/media-codec-profile/profile.env), which carries their
# rationale — the MH buffer-latency coupling, the do-not-unify constants and
# the frame-rate bound — corrected for MH now READING those values.
#
# WHAT IS TRUE NOW: the CURRENT image still READS, VALIDATES and DIRECTS these
# three — a bad value still fails startup, and the directive clients receive
# carries these values, not the profile's. Change them only together with the
# profile keys until MC-A lands. Reads removed by MC-A; after that nothing
# reads them.
#
# WHY THEY ARE STILL HERE: two-deploy retirement, as for MC_MAX_RECEIVE_SLOTS
# above (refs `optional: true`; scheduled removal in docs/TODO.md §Media Path
# Obligations, "Delete the story-3 retired config keys").
MC_AUDIO_CODEC=opus
MC_AUDIO_MAX_BITRATE_BPS=48000
MC_AUDIO_FRAME_RATE_HZ=50
# ==========================================================================
# Meeting KEK lifecycle (ADR-0036 §4 Rotation, "Participant leaves") — story
# 2026-09-21-hear-each-other R-12; manifest story task 4, read by MC task 9.
#
# W: the KEK rotation debounce window, in seconds. Every roster removal
# schedules a rotation, debounced to at most once per W measured from the
# OLDEST un-rotated removal. R-12 states the leaver exposure bound AS W.
#
# REQUIRED, NO RUST DEFAULT — the same precedent, for the same reason, as the
# client-facing media signalling keys above: a Rust-defaulted key wired
# into mc-0 and forgotten on mc-1 passes `dt-guard env-config` green while the
# two instances behave differently. For W that divergence is a SECURITY
# defect, not a tuning one: the leaver exposure bound becomes whichever pod
# owns the meeting, and nobody outside can tell which. The documented default
# is the value below — this file is its home, not a constant in config.rs.
#
# W REACHES EVERY PARTICIPANT. MC carries it on the wire as
# `kek_rotation_debounce_seconds` on JoinResponse and MeetingKekUpdate, and
# clients derive their previous-KEK retention from it. The rule lives at
# `JoinResponse.kek_rotation_debounce_seconds` in
# proto/dark_tower/signaling/v1/signaling.proto and is deliberately not
# restated here. So tuning W moves client key hygiene too, not just a server
# timer; and an already-connected client keeps the old W until its next
# MeetingKekUpdate, so server-side rotation and client-side retention disagree
# for a bounded interval by construction.
#
# ORDERING: the ROLLOUT ORDERING / COUPLED SET IS THREE ARTIFACTS block above
# governs this key unchanged (this ConfigMap + BOTH Deployments + the image,
# applied with-or-before each other). The W-specific consequence: a VALUE EDIT
# of W here renames the content-addressed ConfigMap and ROLLS both MC pods, so
# every meeting on them restarts and the new W is in force once the rollout
# completes. Never remove this key in the same deploy that removes its read —
# that is a CrashLoop. (Corrected 2026-10-20 from "a later value edit does not
# roll the pods", which ADR-0038 §2 made false.)
# Enforced by: mc-service, at config load (ConfigError::MissingEnvVar if
# absent; rejects a value outside the bound). The bound's figures and their
# derivation live in crates/mc-service/src/config.rs
# (MIN_/MAX_KEK_ROTATION_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS), deliberately not restated here.
# A missing key is also loud at the Kubernetes layer: both Deployments
# reference it with a non-optional configMapKeyRef, so a pod without it gets
# CreateContainerConfigError.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_meeting_kek_rotation_window_seconds (W as the
# pod loaded it — it must equal the value below on every MC pod), beside
# mc_meeting_kek_rotation_pending_age_seconds and
# mc_meeting_kek_generated_total{trigger}. docs/runbooks/mc-deployment.md
# §Configuration Reference carries its row.
MC_KEK_ROTATION_DEBOUNCE_SECONDS=60
# ---- Connect settle window (ADR-0036 §9; story 2 task 20) ----
# How long MC waits, after the FIRST media-handler connection MH reports for a
# participant, before routing on whatever connections it has observed. It ends
# EARLY the moment the participant is connected to every handler of its
# meeting, so a participant that reaches all handlers pays nothing.
# NOT A DEBOUNCE — do not remove or shrink it to "speed things up". Edges never
# move while both parties can still use them, so without the window a
# participant whose second handler connect lands ~100 ms after its first is
# routed on ONE handler and stays there, and in a fully-connected meeting every
# sender ends up sending to both handlers permanently.
# Derivation of 1500: ~15x the observed gap between one client's parallel
# handler connects as MH reports them in Kind (~100 ms), and it must also cover
# the first-join RegisterMeeting fan-out to every handler — MH only reports a
# connection once the meeting is registered on that handler, so a window
# shorter than that fan-out re-creates the split this window exists to
# prevent. Bounded 100..=10000 at load: below ~100 ms it stops doing its job;
# above 10 s it is a long, silent delay to first audio for anyone who cannot
# reach every handler.
# Cost: time-to-first-audio for a participant that does NOT reach every
# handler is delayed by up to this window. It never appears in join latency
# (mc_session_join_duration_seconds stops at the JoinResponse).
# Enforced by: mc-service, in-process, in the meeting actor. Required, no Rust
# default: a pod without the key crash-loops on MissingEnvVar.
# Where an operator sees it: mc_media_connect_settle_window_seconds (the value
# the pod enforces) and mc_media_connect_settles_total{outcome="window_elapsed"}
# (participants that settled without reaching every handler).
# ORDERING: the ROLLOUT ORDERING / COUPLED SET IS THREE ARTIFACTS block above
# governs this key unchanged. A VALUE EDIT here rolls mc-0 and mc-1 (the
# ConfigMap is content-addressed); check mc_media_connect_settle_window_seconds
# for the window each pod enforces. (Corrected 2026-10-20 from "a later value
# edit does not roll the pods".)
MC_MEDIA_CONNECT_SETTLE_MS=1500
